+### Support by email (Action Mailbox)
+
+A business's inbox is `<subdomain>@<HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN>`, for example
+`acme@support.example.test`. Configure your SMTP relay/MX to accept both these
+addresses and their `+reply-...` variants, preserve the original recipients, and
+POST the complete RFC 822 message to:
+
+```text
+POST /rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+Content-Type: message/rfc822
+Authorization: Basic <actionmailbox:RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD>
+```
+
+For example (use HTTPS outside local development):
+
+```sh
+curl --user "actionmailbox:$RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD" \
+  --header 'Content-Type: message/rfc822' \
+  --data-binary @message.eml \
+  https://api.example.com/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+```
+
+The endpoint is Rails' authenticated **relay ingress**, not a business API route;
+it does not require a bearer token or `X-Helpdesk-Account`. Successful receipt
+returns `204`; invalid credentials return `401`, and a wrong content type returns
+`415`. In test, routing and mail delivery complete before the request returns,
+without a separate worker. Ignored messages silently become `bounced` in Action
+Mailbox; no actual bounce email is sent.
+
+- To, Cc, Bcc, and relay `X-Original-To`/`X-Forwarded-To` recipients are considered,
+  case-insensitively. Mail addressed to more than one known business is ignored,
+  including a mix of business inboxes and private reply addresses. Unknown or
+  cancelled businesses, ambiguous repeated recipient/identity headers, automated
+  mail, and list mail are ignored.
+- A new message to the plain business inbox creates an open ticket using the
+  subject and decoded plain-text message as its first public comment. HTML and
+  attachments are not used as the message. Missing/invalid senders, empty text,
+  and missing/overlong subjects are ignored.
+- The sender's existing active customer is reused **within that business**. New
+  customers are unverified, receive a verification link, and are assigned an
+  unguessable random password. Receipt of email never verifies an account. After
+  verification, they can use the existing password-reset flow to set a password
+  and sign in to the portal. Staff/suspended users and addresses reserved by a
+  staff invitation are not registered as customers.
+- Only a staff **public comment** on an email-origin ticket sends a notification
+  to its requester, from the business inbox, with a private per-ticket
+  `Reply-To: acme+reply-<random-token>@support.example.test`. Private notes,
+  status-only updates, customer comments, and portal-origin tickets send none.
+  Notifications are enqueued after the comment transaction commits.
+- Replies to the private address are accepted only when the `From` address matches
+  the ticket's active customer requester. They append a public comment and reopen
+  pending or solved tickets without changing the subject. Invalid tokens,
+  cross-business tokens, and multiple ticket tokens are ignored; they never fall
+  back to creating a new ticket. A new message to the **plain** inbox always opens
+  a new ticket, even with a matching subject or `In-Reply-To` header.
+- Accepted deliveries are deduplicated globally by Message-ID, with a PostgreSQL
+  unique constraint protecting concurrent deliveries. Validation and the
+  acceptance marker are in the same transaction, so invalid deliveries do not
+  prevent corrected (or identical, after the business is restored) retries. The
+  acceptance ledger survives Action Mailbox's normal raw-email incineration. The
+  Rails ingress checksum index is intentionally non-unique: deduplication happens
+  only after validation. Missing Message-IDs use Rails' checksum-based fallback.
+
+Raw mail is stored through Active Storage (`storage/` locally; `tmp/storage/` in
+test). Mount persistent/shared storage in production or configure an appropriate
+Active Storage service. Configure a **durable Active Job adapter and its workers**
+for production routing, outgoing notifications, and delayed raw-email cleanup;
+Rails' default in-process async adapter is suitable only for local development.
+The SMTP service must permit mail from the configured support domain. The relay
+is a trusted boundary: accept mail through a properly configured mail server,
+apply your usual spam/authentication checks there, and do not expose its password
+or private ticket reply addresses publicly.
